9. Concurrent Load Testing (SRE Validation)

Single-request latency testing is useful, but production endpoints must also handle concurrent requests. This test measures endpoint behavior under parallel load.

Option 1: Simple Bash Concurrent Test

Run 20 requests with 5 concurrent workers.
#!/bin/bash

ENDPOINT_ID="<ENDPOINT_ID>"
REGION="us-central1"
REQUEST_FILE="request.json"

echo "Starting concurrent load test..."

seq 20 | xargs -I{} -P 5 \
bash -c '
START=$(date +%s%3N)

gcloud ai endpoints predict '"$ENDPOINT_ID"' \
  --region='"$REGION"' \
  --json-request='"$REQUEST_FILE"' \
  >/dev/null 2>&1

END=$(date +%s%3N)
echo $((END-START))
' > results.txt

echo "Completed."

echo "Total Requests: $(wc -l < results.txt)"
echo "Average Latency: $(awk '{sum+=$1} END {print sum/NR}' results.txt) ms"
echo "Min Latency: $(sort -n results.txt | head -1) ms"
echo "Max Latency: $(sort -n results.txt | tail -1) ms"


Option 2: REST API Load Test Using curl

Avoids the overhead of repeatedly launching the gcloud CLI.

export TOKEN=$(gcloud auth print-access-token)

ENDPOINT_URL="https://us-central1-aiplatform.googleapis.com/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/locations/us-central1/endpoints/ENDPOINT_ID:predict"


Execute:
seq 50 | xargs -n1 -P10 -I{} \
curl -s \
-o /dev/null \
-w "%{time_total}\n" \
-X POST \
-H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
-d @request.json \
$ENDPOINT_URL > latency.txt

Metrics:
awk '
{
  sum+=$1
  values[NR]=$1
}
END {
  print "Requests:", NR
  print "Average:", sum/NR, "seconds"
}
' latency.txt



Option 3: Apache Bench (ab)
Install:
sudo apt install apache2-utils

Test:
ab -n 100 \
-c 10 \
-T application/json \
-H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
-p request.json \
https://us-central1-aiplatform.googleapis.com/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/locations/us-central1/endpoints/ENDPOINT_ID:predict

Parameters:
-n = total requests
-c = concurrent requests


Option 4: Production-Grade Test with hey
wget https://hey-release.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/hey_linux_amd64
chmod +x hey_linux_amd64
sudo mv hey_linux_amd64 /usr/local/bin/hey

Run:
hey \
-n 1000 \
-c 20 \
-m POST \
-H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
-D request.json \
"$ENDPOINT_URL"

